## URL and slug policy

Do not copy the reference encoded `/c/{id}/...` URLs.

Use clean WordPress routes:

```text
/products/
product/{product-slug}/
product-category/{term-slug}/
product-line/{term-slug}/
hair-type/{term-slug}/
hair-concern/{term-slug}/
advice/
advice/{article-slug}/
```

Alternative Bulgarian public slugs are acceptable:

```text
/produkti/
/produkt/{slug}/
/kategoria-produkt/{slug}/
/seria/{slug}/
/tip-kosa/{slug}/
/problem/{slug}/
/saveti/
/saveti/{slug}/
```

Choose one slug strategy and keep it consistent.

## Implementation priority from sitemap

Build in this order:

1. Header, announcement bar, navigation, mega menu, mobile menu.
2. Product CPT and taxonomies.
3. Product listing/archive templates.
4. Product card component.
5. Product detail template.
6. ACF product field groups.
7. Homepage flexible sections.
8. Advice/blog archive and article templates.
9. Brand page and CMS flexible pages.
10. Newsletter and footer.
11. Legal/contact/partners pages.
12. Tracking/GDPR/form protection.

## Agent checklist before coding from sitemap

Before coding a route or component, decide which source pattern it maps to:

```text
Reference /c/...        -> WordPress archive/taxonomy/product listing
Reference /p/...        -> WordPress single catalog product
Reference /blog/...     -> WordPress advice article/archive
Reference /cms/...      -> WordPress page or campaign landing page
Reference /storelocator -> WordPress partners/store-locator page
```

Then remove commerce/auth pieces before implementation.

## Final guardrails

- The sitemap is a structural guide, not a requirement to copy every commerce page.
- Category/listing/product/advice/brand UI should be copied conceptually.
- Checkout, account, cart, loyalty, order, and payment routes must not be created.
- Product pages are catalog pages.
- Products are custom post types.
- Main work is UI and editable content structure.
- Use ACF for flexible sections and product fields.
- Use plain CSS.
- Use semantic server-rendered HTML.
